  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a small-sample laboratory video simulation rather than real-world cycling behavior.",
      "what": "Two controlled video simulation experiments with 67 participants analyzed how cyclists make anticipatory yielding decisions when encountering crossing pedestrians. By measuring keyboard responses (Continue or Brake) to first-person traffic videos, the study tested the effects of kinematics, pedestrian density, and the presence of dedicated cycling lanes. Participants braked less frequently and responded slower in low pedestrian-density environments, when riding in dedicated cycling lanes, and when presented with larger time-to-arrival and distance gaps.",
      "why": "The study isolates how specific spatial and social design factors independently alter cyclist braking decisions, demonstrating that dedicated cycling infrastructure and lower pedestrian densities reduce a cyclist's propensity to yield."
    }
